---
/**
 * foodcraft / CtaBandBN — "Panday Coffee Works" CLOSING CTA BAND
 * (design element data-component="CTA-BAND", "Panday Coffee Works Landing.dc.html"
 * line 294).
 *
 * ROLE: the page's last ask, and the ONE section on this bone sheet whose ground
 * is the rust itself. It renders FOURTEENTH in PageBN's order (…, Location,
 * Area, Faq, CTA-BAND, Footer), and it is the only place the spine's rail runs
 * over a coloured ground rather than over paper. Two cells: the shout on the
 * left (eyebrow + the biggest display line after the h1) and the ask on the
 * right (one sentence and two butted actions).
 *
 * DESIGN VALUES, lifted verbatim from the design element:
 *   section     border-bottom 1px #17181A, background #A8351B
 *                                                    → .sec + --accent
 *               (the design nests its rail grid in an inner div; .sec puts the
 *               same grid on the <section> itself — identical rendering, and it
 *               is what keeps this band inside the wrapper's shared 760 rail
 *               pass instead of re-implementing it)
 *   rail        border-right 1px rgba(239,238,234,.3), flex-end / centre,
 *               padding 20px 0                       → .rail + rgba(--paper-rgb,.3)
 *   rail label  rotated mono 10px / .3em caps, #F6DDD6
 *                                                    → .rail-t + --blush
 *   body cell   padding 72px 34px                    → .sec-body override
 *   grid        minmax(0,1.2fr) minmax(0,.8fr), gap 40px, align-items center
 *   eyebrow     mono 10px / .24em caps, #F6DDD6      → .kick + --blush
 *   h2          800, clamp(34px,5vw,76px), lh .96, -.03em, caps,
 *               margin .14em 0 0, #FBF6F4            → .h2 + --cream
 *   sub         16.5px / 1.76, #F6DDD6, margin 0 0 26px
 *                                                    → --blush
 *   actions     flex, wrap, gap 0 — the two controls BUTT, which is why the
 *               second drops its left border and shares the first's edge
 *   primary     btnCream (design line 414) = mono 10px/.14em caps, 15px 24px,
 *               background #FBF6F4, colour #7C2412, border 1px #FBF6F4
 *                                                    → .btn.btn--cream,
 *               declaration for declaration, so this file adds NO rule for it
 *   secondary   mono 10px/.14em caps, 15px 22px, colour #FBF6F4,
 *               border 1px rgba(251,246,244,.6), border-left none
 *                                                    → .btn + .pdend-tel
 *
 * NO LITERAL HEX IN THIS FILE (R1). #A8351B is --accent, #FBF6F4 is --cream,
 * #F6DDD6 is --blush, #17181A is --rule-strong (the .sec bottom rule) and
 * rgba(239,238,234,.3) is rgba(var(--paper-rgb),.3) exactly.
 *
 * THE ONE COLOUR WITH NO RGB TRIPLET — reached exactly anyway. The secondary's
 * border is rgba(251,246,244,.6), CREAM at 60%, and the wrapper declares no
 * --cream-rgb. `color-mix(in srgb, var(--cream) 60%, transparent)` composites to
 * that value EXACTLY out of the token itself, so the design's own hairline is
 * what gets drawn and no hex is invented. It is preceded by an
 * rgba(var(--paper-rgb),.6) FALLBACK declaration for engines without color-mix,
 * which composites to #D3A497 against the design's #DAA99D — 7/5/6 per channel
 * at a 1px hairline. An earlier draft shipped only that approximation, and it is
 * not a neutral one: it lands on the far side of the 3:1 a UI boundary wants
 * (see CONTRAST below), which is why the exact mix is the value that wins.
 *
 * ── EDITABLE HOOKS (every one DECLARED in
 *    components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts — group "ctaBand", plus one
 *    field from group "location" — so each data-field below is a hook a real
 *    sidebar input owns and focuses) ─────────────────────────────────────────
 *   ctaBand.tag        — the blush eyebrow (.kick)
 *   ctaBand.headline   — the display line, bound with set:html so an owner's own
 *                        <em> takes this design's second voice anywhere inside
 *                        THEIR sentence (see THE SECOND VOICE below)
 *   ctaBand.sub        — the one sentence in the right cell
 *   ctaBand.cta.text + data-href-field ctaBand.cta.href — the cream action
 *   location.phone     — the outlined action (data-field ONLY, see THE PHONE)
 * Nothing else. A data-field on any other path is a dead hook that no sidebar
 * input owns and that focuses nothing, so no other path is written here.
 *
 * ── DERIVED, never stored and never editable ────────────────────────────────
 *   · THE RAIL LABEL "11 / Contact". The spine's index is decorative and derived
 *     per the wrapper contract — never a hook, never stored. 11 is this
 *     section's position among the RAILED sections (the design's own sequence
 *     starts at Hero = "00 / Index" and skips the railless Header, Trust strip
 *     and Footer), and it is the number the design itself prints here. It is
 *     aria-hidden: it is a string this FILE wrote, not the owner's, so it
 *     belongs to the drawing and not to the document outline.
 *   · the CTA's href, resolved through onPage() against the ids PageBN actually
 *     mounts, and its target/rel pair — one conditional per attribute, so
 *     neither is ever emitted twice
 *   · the tel: href, built from the phone's OWN digits
 *   · whether the band is two cells or one — see THE EMPTY CELL
 *
 * ── NOT SUPPLIED BY THIS COMPONENT (R2) ─────────────────────────────────────
 * NOT ONE VISIBLE WORD. The design's demo strings — "Bar is open", "Send the
 * order ahead.", "Message us the drinks and a pickup time and it will be on the
 * bar when you get here. Beans too, ground to your brewer." — are the DESIGNER
 * filling in their own template, and the last of them is a service promise made
 * on behalf of a business nobody has spoken to. A real shop gets a closing band
 * that says only what that shop said, so every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag: no
 * eyebrow over nothing, no display line above an empty cell, no action row with
 * no actions in it, and no <br> where the designer hand-broke their own
 * headline (an owner's sentence breaks where their measure breaks it).
 *
 * ── NOT RENDERED, deliberately ──────────────────────────────────────────────
 *   · ctaBand.image. THE DESIGN GIVES THIS BAND NO PHOTOGRAPH — it is a flat
 *     rust ground, two text cells, nothing else. The schema declares the field
 *     because other templates' bands are full-bleed photographs; here it stays
 *     in the sidebar and is simply unused, which is the honest outcome. Drawing
 *     a .plate nobody asked for would put a grey slab in the middle of the one
 *     section of this sheet that is pure colour.
 *   · No id on the <section>. This page's anchors are menu / about / gallery /
 *     visit / faq / main; the design gives CTA-BAND none of them and no nav link
 *     points at it, so inventing one would add a target nothing aims for. A
 *     derived '#book' resolves to '#main' through ANCHOR_ALIAS below.
 *   · No aria-label on the <section>. Naming it would make it a landmark
 *     announced with a word I wrote; the h2 already titles the region.
 *   · No star row, no rating, no count, no figure of any kind. Nothing in this
 *     band is a claim, and the ctaBand group declares no numeric field to draw
 *     one from honestly, so there is nothing here to fabricate.
 *   · No role="img" and no .plate anywhere — there is no upload in this section.
 *   · No target="_blank" on the cream action unless the href is really off-site.
 *     The design hard-codes one because its demo href is an external messaging
 *     URL, but the same button resolves to '#visit' on a stock build and opening
 *     an in-page anchor in a new tab is a bug. Emitted for http(s) ONLY.
 *   · No .lede / .body primitive on the sub-line. Both are --ink-soft, a
 *     paper-ground colour that is 1.02:1 against this rust and unreadable on it;
 *     opting in and then overriding colour, size, leading and measure would be
 *     more code, not less.
 *
 * ── SECTION GATE (R5) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The band renders when it has BODY content: an ACTION (the CTA label or a
 * phone number) or the SENTENCE. It deliberately does NOT count the eyebrow or
 * the headline — lib/derive-content-defaults.ts fills ctaBand.headline with
 * "Ready when you are." on EVERY site, so a gate that counted it would be a
 * no-op that ships a display line over an empty rust slab. derive also fills
 * ctaBand.cta.text ('Get in touch') unconditionally, which is why the band does
 * in practice render on a stock build — but that is a real, working action
 * pointed at a real id on this page, not a decoration, and an owner who clears
 * it and supplies no phone and no sentence drops the whole <section> from the
 * document. (derive fills neither ctaBand.tag nor ctaBand.sub, so both are
 * genuinely absent until an owner types them.)
 *
 * ── THE EMPTY CELL ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The design is two cells because the designer filled both. The LEFT one is
 * pure display — eyebrow and headline — and an owner can clear both, so the
 * grid drops to a single 1fr track whenever the left cell is empty. Without
 * that, the sentence and the buttons would sit in the right-hand .8fr track
 * with 1.2fr of bare rust beside them, which reads as a rendering failure
 * rather than as a deliberate surface. The right cell cannot be empty and still
 * reach the DOM — it IS the section gate.
 *
 * ── THE SECOND VOICE, and this is the decision most worth reviewing ─────────
 * This family's headline emphasis is a COLOUR CHANGE, and PageBN paints
 * `:where(.h2 em)` --accent from its global block. On this ONE band --accent is
 * the GROUND, so an owner's emphasised words would disappear into it entirely.
 * The em is therefore remapped, through :global() because a scoped rule cannot
 * reach an element set:html created (R3), to --blush — the band's own quiet
 * colour, already carrying the eyebrow, the sub-line and the rail label.
 * BE HONEST ABOUT WHAT THAT BUYS: computed from the wrapper's hexes, --blush on
 * --accent is 5.09:1 (readable, above AA) but --blush against the --cream
 * headline around it is only 1.21:1 — a hue shift from warm white to pink, not
 * a strong tonal break. That is the ceiling this ground has: every token light
 * enough to read on rust is a near-white, and the two that are not (--orange
 * 2.07:1, --ink 2.70:1 on --accent) are both below AA as text here. The design
 * never draws an em on this band, so there is no drawn answer to copy. Keeping
 * the words READABLE beat making them louder.
 *
 * ── THE PHONE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * THE PHONE ANCHOR CARRIES data-field ONLY — no data-href-field. Its href is
 * DERIVED from the field's own value, so a data-href-field there would write
 * "tel:+63…" back into the phone field on the first inline edit; V3 also bails
 * out of inline editing on tel:/mailto: anchors and on anything carrying
 * data-href-field. This is the family's single documented exception to "every
 * anchor gets both".
 *
 * ITS READ CHAIN is content.location.phone || content.contact.phone, which is
 * exactly what the sidebar's Location → Phone input reads (the schema declares
 * location.phone with fallbackPaths ['contact.phone'] and writes back to the
 * primary), so the rendered number and the field this hook opens are always the
 * same value. layout.contact.phone — the display-formatted mirror the builder
 * derives — is deliberately NOT preferred: astro-builder formats
 * content.contact.phone and ignores location.phone entirely, so on a site whose
 * owner typed a number into Location it would render a DIFFERENT number from
 * the one the hook edits.
 *
 * WHY A location.* HOOK IS SAFE HERE. editorBridge scores every candidate
 * <section> by how many location.* hooks it actually contains, hides the
 * richest, and keeps the FIRST host to reach that count (`counts[k] > bestN`,
 * over hosts collected in document order). COUNTED against the siblings rather
 * than assumed: LocationBN emits location.headline, location.sub and
 * location.hours, plus TWO more per hours row (location.rules.<i>.label and
 * .value); FooterBN emits three (location.phone once, location.address twice);
 * this band emits ONE. So "hide LOCATION" resolves to LocationBN whenever it
 * renders — on count, and on document order if the count ever ties — never here.
 * (LocationBN prints no hook at all for address, lat or lng: it READS them for
 * the geocode and leaves each to its own sidebar input, so the census is three
 * plus the rows, not the whole group.)
 * THE ONE RESIDUAL CASE, stated rather than waved through: LocationBN drops out
 * of the document entirely when an owner has no arrival note, no hours line, no
 * hours rows and neither address nor coordinates. On such a site, if the footer
 * also carries no address, its lone phone hook ties this band's at 1 — and this
 * band renders first, so the toggle would hide the closing band. That is a site
 * with no location block for the toggle to hide in the first place.
 *
 * ── ACCESSIBILITY (R9) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The rail index is aria-hidden — a decorative numeral this file wrote. The
 * tel: link's accessible name is the owner's own number and nothing else, with
 * no "Call …" prefix I invented; the cream action's name is the owner's own
 * label. No element in this file carries both a literal and a conditional
 * aria-*, so nothing here can emit a duplicated attribute.
 * FOCUS is left to the wrapper and NOT overridden, which is a choice rather
 * than an omission: its ring is a 3px --paper outline inside a 6px --accent
 * halo, and on this band the halo IS the ground and vanishes. What remains — a
 * 3px paper line — is 5.67:1 against the rust, comfortably past the 3:1 a focus
 * indicator needs. The alternatives lose here, though not all of them on ground
 * contrast: an --ink halo is 2.70:1 on --accent, below the bar, but --cream
 * measures 6.14:1 on this rust — HIGHER than paper's 5.67 — and --blush 5.09:1.
 * Those two are ruled out by the RING, not by the ground: against the --paper
 * outline they would surround they are 1.08:1 and 1.11:1, a merge rather than a
 * duo. So 5.67 is not this ground's ceiling — it is the strongest indicator that
 * survives here WITHOUT overriding the wrapper's ring, which is the choice made.
 *
 * ── CONTRAST, COMPUTED (sRGB relative luminance, WCAG 2.x) from the hexes
 *    PageBN declares — not estimated, and re-derived here rather than copied,
 *    with --paper on --accent landing on the wrapper's own published 5.67 as a
 *    check on the arithmetic ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *   --cream headline / outlined label on --accent      6.14:1
 *   --paper (the focus ring) on --accent               5.67:1
 *   --blush eyebrow / sub-line / rail label / em       5.09:1
 *   --accent-deep label on the --cream fill            9.25:1 (the label sits on
 *                                                     cream, not on the band)
 * Every text pair on this band is above AA at any size; the tightest is 5.09.
 * The secondary's 1px border is the design's own rgba(251,246,244,.6), which
 * composites over this ground to #DAA99D and measures 3.18:1 — past the 3:1 a
 * UI boundary wants. Only the pre-color-mix FALLBACK composites to #D3A497 at
 * 2.99:1, just under it, and even there the control is identified by its --cream
 * label at 6.14:1 rather than by the hairline. (Those last two are COMPOSITED
 * colours, not declared tokens, so they are the only figures on this page that
 * cannot be looked up in the wrapper's token-pair table.)
 * SCHEME CAVEAT, stated because the figures above are only true of the native
 * palette: --accent is engine-owned and a scheme CAN move this band's ground,
 * while --cream and --blush are invisible to buildOverrideCss and cannot follow
 * it. That is PageBN's deliberate arrangement ("its label colours must not drift
 * with it"), not this file's, and it is why the labels here are --cream/--blush
 * rather than --on-accent.
 *
 * ── CLASS-NAME LAW (R4, not inferable, do not "tidy") ───────────────────────
 * The section is .pdend, NEVER .cta-band: buildOverrideCss emits
 * `.cta-band .btn { color: <on-ink> !important }` for every state and would
 * strand both labels here. Never .btn-primary / .btn-ghost / .btn-yellow /
 * .btn-light / .nav-cta / .nav-right / .trust / .testi in this family, and never
 * `.hero` on a <section>. The prefix is `pdend-`, this template's own and shared
 * with nothing: CtaBandBM's `tcend-` names were NOT carried over — copying a
 * sibling's class names verbatim is the exact mistake an audit had to unpick on
 * the template before this one — and it collides with none of the BN siblings
 * already shipped (pcw-, pcwg-, pcwm-, pcwq-, pdcr-, pd-ar-, pdw-, hbn-,
 * pcbn-).
 *
 * ── R3 / SCOPING ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * One scoped <style> block, no is:global. Astro 5 scopes with
 * [data-astro-cid-…], an attribute selector worth (0,1,0), so a single-class
 * rule below scores (0,2,0) and outruns the wrapper's `html[data-page="BN"]
 * :where(…)` primitives at (0,1,1) — EVEN INSIDE the wrapper's own media
 * queries, which is why the shared 760 h2 safeties and the shared 520 button
 * padding are restated below rather than left to land on their own. No
 * !important appears anywhere in this file.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBN passes it to every section uniformly;
// this block reads nothing outside content.*.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any;
}
const { content } = Astro.props;


// ── ANCHOR GUARD (R7) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Resolve in-page hrefs against the ids PageBN ACTUALLY renders: keep one when
// the id exists, remap it when this design carries the same block under another
// name, otherwise fall back to the target the design was built around. Off-page
// hrefs — tel:, mailto:, http(s) and anything else without a leading '#' — pass
// through untouched. lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes
// ctaBand.cta.href = '#visit' and this page DOES draw '#visit', so on a stock
// build the fallback never fires — but an owner (or a future derive) can type
// any of the aliases below.
// Keep PAGE_ANCHORS and ANCHOR_ALIAS in step with PageBN and with the copies in
// the other BN sections. There is no shared import by design (a section
// component in this repo receives only `content` + `layout`); the copies must
// stay identical by hand.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'visit', 'faq', 'main']);
// This design renames six derived targets: the services block IS the menu
// (rooms lands there too), '#about' is the roastery's own story (host and why
// are the same block here), '#work' is the photo wall, and '#location' /
// '#contact' both mean the visit block — address, map and opening hours. A
// booking link has no checkout to reach on this page, so it lands on <main>.
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'visit',
  host: 'about',
  location: 'visit',
  rooms: 'menu',
  services: 'menu',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

const c = (content as any) || {};
const cta = c.ctaBand || {};

// `||`, never `??`: an owner who CLEARS a box leaves '' behind, and the sidebar
// treats '' as empty too. With `??` the empty string would count as a value and
// the element would render blank instead of being dropped.
const tag = String(cta.tag || '').trim();
// Trimmed and otherwise untouched: this goes out through set:html, so whatever
// markup the owner typed reaches the em rule intact.
const headline = String(cta.headline || '').trim();
const sub = String(cta.sub || '').trim();

const ctaText = String(cta.cta?.text || '').trim();
// '#visit' is the fallback because that is where this design puts the address,
// the map and the hours — the thing a closing ask points at.
const ctaHref = onPage(cta.cta?.href, '#visit');
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);

// See THE PHONE'S READ CHAIN in the doc comment.
const phone = String(c.location?.phone || c.contact?.phone || '').trim();
const telHref = phone ? `tel:${phone.replace(/[^0-9+]/g, '')}` : '';

const hasActions = Boolean(ctaText || phone);
// The left cell is pure display; the right cell is the ask.
const hasLead = Boolean(tag || headline);
const hasAsk = Boolean(sub || hasActions);
// SECTION GATE (R5) — body content only: an action or the sentence. Neither the
// eyebrow nor the headline counts; see the doc comment.
const hasBand = hasAsk;
// One track whenever the display cell is empty; see THE EMPTY CELL.
const solo = !hasLead;
---
{hasBand && (
<section class="sec pdend">
  <div class="rail pdend-rail">
    {/* DERIVED chrome: the spine's index. No data-field, never stored. */}
    <span class="rail-t pdend-railt" aria-hidden="true">Contact</span>
  </div>
  <div class="sec-body pdend-body">
    <div class:list={['pdend-grid', solo && 'pdend-grid--solo']}>
      {hasLead && (
        <div class="pdend-lead reveal">
          {/* .kick ALONE for size, tracking and face — only the colour changes,
              because --accent is this band's ground. */}
          {tag && <span class="kick pdend-kick" data-field="ctaBand.tag">{tag}</span>}
          {/* set:html so the owner's own <em> lands where THEY put it in THEIR
              sentence; the blush second voice is the :global() rule below (R3). */}
          {headline && <h2 class="h2 pdend-h" data-field="ctaBand.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
        </div>
      )}
      <div class="pdend-ask reveal" data-delay="1">
        {sub && <p class="pdend-sub" data-field="ctaBand.sub">{sub}</p>}
        {hasActions && (
          <div class="pdend-acts">
            {ctaText && (
              <a
                class="btn btn--cream"
                href={ctaHref}
                target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
                rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
                data-field="ctaBand.cta.text"
                data-href-field="ctaBand.cta.href"
              >{ctaText}</a>
            )}
            {/* data-field ONLY — no data-href-field. See THE PHONE. */}
            {phone && (
              <a class="btn pdend-tel" href={telHref} data-field="location.phone">{phone}</a>
            )}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
)}
<style>
  /* The spine (.sec / .rail / .rail-t / .sec-body), the display face, the caps,
     the tracking and the whole cream button all arrive from PageBN's
     primitives. What this file owns is THE COLOURED GROUND: the rust fill, the
     hairline that has to change because of it, and every text colour that has
     to change because of it. Nothing else is restated.

     No literal end-of-style token appears anywhere in these comments — this is
     a raw-text element, and one inside a comment would end the stylesheet here
     and silently truncate the rest of the file. */

  /* THE ONE COLOURED SECTION. The design paints this ground #A8351B and leaves
     the spine's bottom rule (--rule-strong) in place, so the band's lower edge
     is drawn against the footer beneath it. `color` is set on the section as
     well as on every child: a colour leaking in from the wrapper would be --ink
     text on a rust ground at 2.70:1. */
  .pdend {
    background-color: var(--accent);
    color: var(--cream);
  }

  /* The rail's hairlines. The wrapper draws them in --line (#C8C7C2), a
     PAPER-ground grey that reads as a bright stripe across rust; the design uses
     rgba(239,238,234,.3), which is --paper-rgb at 30% exactly. Both edges are
     set in one rule because the wrapper's shared 760 pass swaps border-right for
     border-bottom — above 760 the bottom border is 0-width and its colour is
     inert, so this needs no media query of its own. */
  .pdend-rail {
    border-right-color: rgba(var(--paper-rgb), .3);
    border-bottom-color: rgba(var(--paper-rgb), .3);
  }

  /* --blush, not --accent: the wrapper's rail label is the accent, which IS this
     ground. 5.09:1 here. */
  .pdend-railt { color: var(--blush); }

  /* Design: padding 72px 34px — symmetric, where the wrapper's shared cell is
     44/34/40. Only the VERTICAL value is this section's; the horizontal clamp is
     the wrapper's own, character for character, so the rust band's text edge
     lines up with the bone sections above it. Any other value here would put a
     visible jog in the left margin of the sheet. */
  .pdend-body {
    padding: clamp(44px, 6.2vw, 72px) clamp(20px, 3vw, 34px);
  }

  /* Design: minmax(0,1.2fr) minmax(0,.8fr), gap 40px, align-items center.
     minmax(0,…) on both tracks so a long unbroken owner word — a headline, a
     phone number — cannot widen its own track and push the page sideways (R8). */
  .pdend-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.2fr) minmax(0, .8fr);
    gap: 40px;
    align-items: center;
  }
  /* No display cell ⇒ one track. See THE EMPTY CELL in the frontmatter. */
  .pdend-grid--solo { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }

  /* A grid item's default min-width is auto, which the minmax() above cannot
     override on its own. */
  .pdend-lead,
  .pdend-ask { min-width: 0; }

  /* The eyebrow is owner-typed and set in 10px caps at .24em tracking: one
     unbroken word would otherwise be the widest thing in its cell (R8). Only
     the COLOUR is this file's — size, face and tracking are .kick's. */
  .pdend-kick {
    color: var(--blush);
    max-width: 100%;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Design: clamp(34px,5vw,76px) / .96, margin .14em 0 0, #FBF6F4, no measure
     cap. The wrapper's .h2 is clamp(28px,4.4vw,62px)/.98 in --ink at 20ch, and
     its shared 760 pass also carries `max-width:none` and
     `overflow-wrap:anywhere`. This rule outruns BOTH (see SCOPING), so those two
     safeties are restated here unconditionally rather than lost — they cost
     nothing at any width and they are what stops a long unbroken owner word at
     360px. */
  .pdend-h {
    font-size: clamp(34px, 5vw, 76px);
    line-height: .96;
    margin: .14em 0 0;
    color: var(--cream);
    max-width: none;
